skinshots - before/after screenshots of the built-in theme on every screen size

  utils/skinshots/skinshots.py --work DIR COMMIT1 COMMIT2 [...]

Checks each commit out in a private worktree (DIR/tree-<sha>; your own
checkout is never touched) and patches it with simhack.py (TEST ONLY hooks:
screendump and exit a few seconds after boot, autoplay of a generated
test.wav, fixed peak meter levels, frozen clock). Then, several targets at a
time (-p), each target from targets.txt is configured and built once - only
the binary and codecs, no plugins - and shot at every commit in turn: between
commits the build's source symlink is repointed at the next worktree and make
rebuilds only what differs (just "make install" when only themes changed).
Each commit's screens - WPS, and recording and FM radio where the target has
them, the remote LCD along with each - are shot at once, every simulator on
its own copy of simdisk. The build is deleted as soon as its target is done,
the worktrees at the end. Builds go through a short /tmp/ssXXXX symlink, as
make's dependency step overflows the command line under long paths.

Then draws one sheet per target: a column per commit, plus a diff column
(changed pixels magenta over the last commit, dimmed), and prints a summary.

  DIR/out/shots/<sha>/<target>/*.png   the screenshots
  DIR/out/collage/<WxHxD>-<target>.png  the sheets; all.png all of them
  DIR/out/index.html                    the sheets on one page
  DIR/out/logs/<sha>/<target>/          configure, make, run logs

Rerunning skips what's already shot, so after a fix only the missing or
failed (--retry-failed) targets are built; --force reshoots all,
--collage-only just redraws the sheets.

  --only t1,t2        these targets instead of targets.txt
  --targets FILE      another target list
  -p N                targets built at once (default 3)
  -j N                make jobs per build, capped by load (default: CPU count)
  --delay N           seconds after boot to take the shots (default 4)
  --min-free-gb N     stop before a build when less is free (default 5)
  --min-width N       upscale sheets so the narrowest shot is N px wide

Needs Python 3 with PIL, the host gcc and SDL2 dev files the simulators need.
